\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.1}Layers and ports}{9}{subsection.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The layer model and the hexagonal ports. Line counts are measured at \texttt  {\small  0f577b15} (\texttt  {\small  crates/<name>/src/**/*.rs}; \texttt  {\small  kayfabe-abi}'s figure excludes its detached offline generator, 3\,284 lines). \textbf  {★★★ The bands inverted at this revision}: L2 and L3 were drawn as dashed {\color  {kfhostln}\textbf  {\textsf  {NOT BUILT}}}{} boxes at the previous pin and are now built, shipped and booted against. Implementor census, counted from \texttt  {\small  impl <Trait> for} across the tree and re-derived rather than carried over: \texttt  {\small  Arch} has \textbf  {three} production implementations (\texttt  {\small  Ga10xArch}, \texttt  {\small  Ad10xArch}, \texttt  {\small  Gh100Arch} in \texttt  {\small  kayfabe-chips}); \texttt  {\small  Isolate}, \texttt  {\small  IsolateFactory} and \texttt  {\small  RmBackend} have real ones in \texttt  {\small  kayfabe-isolate-host}; \texttt  {\small  Vmm} has \emph  {two} real adapters (\texttt  {\small  KvmVmm}, \texttt  {\small  QemuVmm}); \texttt  {\small  FbRead}, which the previous revision recorded as having \emph  {no implementation of any kind}, has two; \texttt  {\small  GuestRam} has a production implementor (\texttt  {\small  MachineRam}, in \texttt  {\small  kayfabe-qemu-raw}). \textbf  {\texttt  {\small  Device} is the one port on this diagram whose only implementors are still test types} — the QEMU shell reaches the core through \texttt  {\small  GuestRam} and the register plane instead.}}{10}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:layers}{{1}{10}{The layer model and the hexagonal ports. Line counts are measured at \code {0f577b15} (\code {crates/<name>/src/**/*.rs}; \code {kayfabe-abi}'s figure excludes its detached offline generator, 3\,284 lines). \textbf {★★★ The bands inverted at this revision}: L2 and L3 were drawn as dashed \unbuilt {} boxes at the previous pin and are now built, shipped and booted against. Implementor census, counted from \code {impl <Trait> for} across the tree and re-derived rather than carried over: \code {Arch} has \textbf {three} production implementations (\code {Ga10xArch}, \code {Ad10xArch}, \code {Gh100Arch} in \code {kayfabe-chips}); \code {Isolate}, \code {IsolateFactory} and \code {RmBackend} have real ones in \code {kayfabe-isolate-host}; \code {Vmm} has \emph {two} real adapters (\code {KvmVmm}, \code {QemuVmm}); \code {FbRead}, which the previous revision recorded as having \emph {no implementation of any kind}, has two; \code {GuestRam} has a production implementor (\code {MachineRam}, in \code {kayfabe-qemu-raw}). \textbf {\code {Device} is the one port on this diagram whose only implementors are still test types} — the QEMU shell reaches the core through \code {GuestRam} and the register plane instead}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}The crate dependency graph}{11}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The crate dependency matrix, re-derived from scratch by script from the manifests at \texttt  {\small  0f577b15} — \textbf  {24 members}, \textbf  {134} \texttt  {\small  [dependencies]} edges and 9 \texttt  {\small  [dev-dependencies]} edges that are not already hard edges. \texttt  {\small  kayfabe-qemu-raw} (row 23) is the row that matters: it is the composition root, the \texttt  {\small  staticlib} a C QOM shim links into a real QEMU, and it depends on \emph  {eighteen} of the twenty-two crates above it. \textbf  {★ The previous revision's row ordering is no longer topological} — \texttt  {\small  qemu-raw} has since acquired a dependency on \texttt  {\small  shell}, which sat below it — so \texttt  {\small  shell} and \texttt  {\small  qemu-raw} are swapped here and the strictly-lower-triangular property (which \emph  {is} the acyclicity proof) holds again. Drawn in the old order the matrix would have carried one dot above the diagonal and nothing in the build would have said so.}}{12}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:deps}{{2}{12}{The crate dependency matrix, re-derived from scratch by script from the manifests at \code {0f577b15} — \textbf {24 members}, \textbf {134} \code {[dependencies]} edges and 9 \code {[dev-dependencies]} edges that are not already hard edges. \code {kayfabe-qemu-raw} (row 23) is the row that matters: it is the composition root, the \code {staticlib} a C QOM shim links into a real QEMU, and it depends on \emph {eighteen} of the twenty-two crates above it. \textbf {★ The previous revision's row ordering is no longer topological} — \code {qemu-raw} has since acquired a dependency on \code {shell}, which sat below it — so \code {shell} and \code {qemu-raw} are swapped here and the strictly-lower-triangular property (which \emph {is} the acyclicity proof) holds again. Drawn in the old order the matrix would have carried one dot above the diagonal and nothing in the build would have said so}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.3}The data path}{13}{subsection.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The data path, verified function by function against the tree at \texttt  {\small  0f577b15}. Numbered steps are the execution plane; \textbf  {C} is the control plane, which populates the address table from declared protocol facts. Boxes name \emph  {symbols}, not line numbers: the repository converted its own 105 pins to symbols in \texttt  {\small  3fd1455} after they drifted. \textbf  {What changed at this revision is that most of the red went green} — the guest band now really does feed the emulated-device band, the isolate band has a real \texttt  {\small  RmBackend}, and steps 7--9 have executed on silicon. \textbf  {The red that is left is the joint}: the doorbell box, which is built, wired and has never been rung by a guest. Red text marks what does not exist or has not happened.}}{14}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:datapath}{{3}{14}{The data path, verified function by function against the tree at \code {0f577b15}. Numbered steps are the execution plane; \textbf {C} is the control plane, which populates the address table from declared protocol facts. Boxes name \emph {symbols}, not line numbers: the repository converted its own 105 pins to symbols in \code {3fd1455} after they drifted. \textbf {What changed at this revision is that most of the red went green} — the guest band now really does feed the emulated-device band, the isolate band has a real \code {RmBackend}, and steps 7--9 have executed on silicon. \textbf {The red that is left is the joint}: the doorbell box, which is built, wired and has never been rung by a guest. Red text marks what does not exist or has not happened}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}The isolation model}{15}{subsection.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The process and isolation model, and the problem it exists to solve. The per-\texttt  {\small  Proc} ownership of all four planes is built and heavily tested against mocks. \textbf  {★ The sandboxed isolate process at the bottom is now real} — capability-less, six fresh namespaces, holding \texttt  {\footnotesize  /dev/nvidiactl} and \texttt  {\footnotesize  /dev/nvidia0}, spawned in response to the guest's own action. ⊘ \textbf  {But the multi-\texttt  {\small  Proc} property this figure exists to argue is \emph  {unmeasured} on hardware}: every bench arm to date has exactly one \texttt  {\small  Proc}, because the guest boot never reaches a second guest process. The picture is proven against mocks and exercised on hardware one \texttt  {\small  Proc} wide.}}{16}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:isolation}{{4}{16}{The process and isolation model, and the problem it exists to solve. The per-\code {Proc} ownership of all four planes is built and heavily tested against mocks. \textbf {★ The sandboxed isolate process at the bottom is now real} — capability-less, six fresh namespaces, holding \file {/dev/nvidiactl} and \file {/dev/nvidia0}, spawned in response to the guest's own action. ⊘ \textbf {But the multi-\code {Proc} property this figure exists to argue is \emph {unmeasured} on hardware}: every bench arm to date has exactly one \code {Proc}, because the guest boot never reaches a second guest process. The picture is proven against mocks and exercised on hardware one \code {Proc} wide}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.5}The lifecycle}{17}{subsection.4.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The eight teardown triggers and the retire/reap split. Two of the eight are honestly broken and say so: T3's quiesce trigger is designed but nothing arms it, and T4's obvious trigger was measured on hardware and does not fire.}}{18}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lifecycle}{{5}{18}{The eight teardown triggers and the retire/reap split. Two of the eight are honestly broken and say so: T3's quiesce trigger is designed but nothing arms it, and T4's obvious trigger was measured on hardware and does not fire}{figure.5}{}}
